    "body": "So the context of this is that I'm working on some new HTML parser scheduling heuristics, one of the behaviours is that it used to yield after processing every <script> tag (it did this so that layout/paint could happen faster). Unfortunately, this broke some poorly coded websites that use <script async>.\r\n\r\nThe one that the issue mentions was doing something like this:\r\n```\r\n<script>\r\nwindow.vanilla.addJs({\"id\":\"main-js\",\"src\":\"\\/\\/vanilla.futurecdn.net\\/tomshardware\\/267900\\/media\\/js\\/main.min.js\"}, null, 0)\r\n</script>  <script>\r\nwindow.ffte = {\"site\":\"tomshardware\", (snip)\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat was happening was that the site author assumed that the second <script> tag would run before the one added in the first (<script> tags added to the document by other scripts are async), but under Chromium's proposed new parser that wasn't necessarily the case. Of course - you're not supposed to assume anything about when async scripts are executed, but this was breaking some sites so we opted for this solution to try and make the new parser act a bit more like the old one while we continue experimenting. There is a [follow up issue open](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=1204178&q=owner%3Arichard.townsend%40arm.com&can=2) to remove the behaviour.\r\n\r\nSo TL;DR - I don't think this is a spec issue, the new parser actually gets a bit closer to what the spec says, we just have to find the sites out there that break and get them fixed.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
